WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.832 --> 00:00:12.933
Jesse dropped his backpack on the kitchen counter with a sigh.

2
00:00:14.094 --> 00:00:15.894
His mother looked up from her laptop.

3
00:00:15.914 --> 00:00:17.575
"Bad day?"

4
00:00:17.595 --> 00:00:20.556
"Sort of," Jesse muttered.

5
00:00:20.576 --> 00:00:26.099
"Career Day is next week, and the principal selected me to introduce one of the speakers.

6
00:00:27.139 --> 00:00:32.554
It's an honor to get selected, but I wanted to introduce the professional soccer player, not Dr.

7
00:00:32.594 --> 00:00:35.615
Devarti." "Really?"

8
00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:37.776
Jesse's mom sounded surprised.

9
00:00:38.756 --> 00:00:44.779
"Dr. Devarti has a fascinating career with Doctors Without Borders—and you've known her for years."

10
00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:48.160
"I know her from our neighborhood," Jesse said.

11
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.162
"But I don't know anything about her career."

12
00:00:51.182 --> 00:00:54.583
"Why don't you give her a call and see if you can interview her?"

13
00:00:55.023 --> 00:00:56.064
his mom suggested.

14
00:00:58.348 --> 00:01:01.709
Thirty minutes later Jesse found himself trudging toward Dr.

15
00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:02.529
Devarti's house.

16
00:01:02.549 --> 00:01:05.710
"Come on in," Dr.

17
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:06.370
Devarti said.

18
00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:11.252
"My kids are working on their homework, so it's a convenient time to talk."

19
00:01:15.713 --> 00:01:19.314
room," Jesse thought as he entered a spacious family room.

20
00:01:20.394 --> 00:01:25.196
Tall bookshelves stuffed with books and interesting objects stretched from floor to ceiling.

21
00:01:27.176 --> 00:01:32.398
Other shelves held African masks, bronze bells, and dolls dressed in costumes.

22
00:01:33.658 --> 00:01:35.779
He picked up a colorful woven basket.

23
00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:39.820
"That was made by a woman in Sudan," Dr.

24
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:40.560
Devarti said.

25
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:46.702
"My first posting after joining Doctors Without Borders was in Darfur, Sudan."

26
00:01:46.722 --> 00:01:51.564
"Thanks for letting me ask you about Doctors Without Borders," Jesse began.

27
00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:57.454
"I need to write a brief introduction before you speak at our Career Day assembly.

28
00:01:58.115 --> 00:02:00.016
So I'd like to get some background information."

29
00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:02.237
"Sure," Dr.

30
00:02:02.257 --> 00:02:02.978
Devarti said.

31
00:02:02.998 --> 00:02:09.702
"Working for Doctors Without Borders has changed my life—and even the way that I practice medicine."

32
00:02:10.923 --> 00:02:16.267
Jesse took notes as the physician explained that Doctors Without Borders had been founded in 1971 in France.

33
00:02:19.964 --> 00:02:25.626
A group of doctors and journalists were troubled by the way politics sometimes interfered with aid for victims

34
00:02:25.686 --> 00:02:26.986
of disaster and disease.

35
00:02:28.887 --> 00:02:34.689
So the group decided to create a new relief organization that could deliver free and rapid medical care

36
00:02:34.729 --> 00:02:39.870
to people affected by war, epidemics, and disasters.

37
00:02:40.011 --> 00:02:43.992
In 1999 the organization received the Nobel Peace Prize.

38
00:02:45.852 --> 00:02:52.794
"Doctors Without Borders is known as Médecins Sans Frontières in some parts of the world," Dr.

39
00:02:52.834 --> 00:02:53.734
Devarti went on.

40
00:02:53.754 --> 00:03:01.636
"It's an impartial humanitarian organization, which means we treat people without considering things like race, religion, or politics."

41
00:03:02.677 --> 00:03:04.157
"Are all the workers volunteers?"

42
00:03:04.557 --> 00:03:05.337
Jesse asked.

43
00:03:07.138 --> 00:03:11.759
"No, everyone has to apply to work for Doctors Without Borders—and get hired.

44
00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:15.100
You're paid a salary, and there are some requirements.

45
00:03:15.948 --> 00:03:20.411
For example, you have to be able to travel on a mission for nine to twelve months at

46
00:03:20.431 --> 00:03:20.891
a time.

47
00:03:21.792 --> 00:03:25.094
You also have to possess the right personality and be flexible.

48
00:03:26.435 --> 00:03:32.419
Workers often face hardships, like living on a boat, in a war zone, or in a city destroyed

49
00:03:32.499 --> 00:03:33.400
by an earthquake.

50
00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:40.144
Doctors and nurses see many heartbreaking situations, so you also have to be able to cope with that.

51
00:03:41.265 --> 00:03:44.167
And it's an asset if you're proficient in more than one language."

52
00:03:44.187 --> 00:03:48.738
"Wow," Jesse said, "that sounds intense."

53
00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:51.959
Dr. Devarti nodded.

54
00:03:51.979 --> 00:03:59.844
"It's dangerous too, especially when you're working in a war zone or treating patients with deadly infectious diseases

55
00:03:59.944 --> 00:04:00.784
like cholera."

56
00:04:02.165 --> 00:04:03.506
Jesse skimmed his notes.

57
00:04:03.526 --> 00:04:08.209
"You said Doctors Without Borders delivers rapid medical care.

58
00:04:08.769 --> 00:04:10.830
But its patients live all over the world.

59
00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.812
How can medical workers get to these places quickly?"

60
00:04:15.592 --> 00:04:16.312
"Good question.

61
00:04:16.733 --> 00:04:21.114
Doctors Without Borders has over thirty thousand workers in about seventy countries.

62
00:04:23.195 --> 00:04:28.477
And there are hubs all over the world, so they can mobilize medical teams quickly and efficiently.

63
00:04:29.458 --> 00:04:36.680
The hubs are stocked with reserves of prepackaged kits containing medicine and supplies—even inflatable tents for surgical procedures."

64
00:04:38.501 --> 00:04:45.184
"Whoa," Jesse said, "you can perform surgery outdoors in the middle of a disaster area?"

65
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.321
Dr. Devarti nodded.

66
00:04:47.341 --> 00:04:49.122
"We sure can.

67
00:04:49.802 --> 00:04:52.084
It allows us to save many lives."

68
00:04:52.104 --> 00:04:55.706
"What was the hardest mission you've ever been on?"

69
00:04:56.046 --> 00:04:56.846
Jesse asked.

70
00:04:56.866 --> 00:04:59.168
"Hmm Dr.

71
00:04:59.208 --> 00:05:00.669
Devarti thought for a moment.

72
00:05:00.689 --> 00:05:06.752
"Last year I worked on a medical boat in the Mediterranean Sea.

73
00:05:07.553 --> 00:05:12.716
Conditions at sea were rough, and we were treating refugees who had fled their home countries by boat.

74
00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.682
Many of our patients were kids whose parents had drowned."

75
00:05:16.702 --> 00:05:21.165
"I see what you mean about heartbreaking," Jesse murmured.

76
00:05:21.185 --> 00:05:25.427
"I can't imagine losing my parents as well as my home.

77
00:05:27.448 --> 00:05:31.731
One final question: what do you like best about working for Doctors Without Borders?"

78
00:05:32.731 --> 00:05:34.092
Dr. Devarti smiled.

79
00:05:34.112 --> 00:05:40.396
"That's an easy one—I've traveled all over the world and met lots of interesting people.

80
00:05:41.004 --> 00:05:47.569
I've also learned to stay calm in medical emergencies and listen carefully, especially to patients from different cultures.

81
00:05:51.012 --> 00:05:56.556
Working for Doctors Without Borders has been very rewarding, and it's made me a much better doctor."

82
00:05:57.797 --> 00:06:01.179
Jesse shook his head, awed by what he'd learned about Dr.

83
00:06:01.219 --> 00:06:02.160
Devarti's career.

84
00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.363
Earlier today he'd been worried about introducing her at the assembly.

85
00:06:07.164 --> 00:06:11.322
Now he was eager to share details about her impressive career!
